style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\">info!</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#96D0FF\">\"{}\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">, line</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">.</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\">as_str</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">());</span></span></code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>heapless</code> collections return <code>Err(())</code> on overflow instead of growing. That error\nis a design decision you have to make explicitly — which is exactly what firmware\nrequires.</p>\n<h2 id=\"panics-are-not-free\">Panics are not free</h2>\n<figure data-rehype-pretty-code-figure=\"\"><pre tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"toml\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\"><code data-language=\"toml\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\" style=\"display: grid;\"><span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\"># a panic handler must exist; choose deliberately</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">panic-halt = </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#96D0FF\">\"0.2\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\">          # infinite loop — the device stops responding</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\"># panic-probe              # log over RTT, then halt — best while developing</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\"># panic-reset              # reboot — right for a watchdog-supervised device</span></span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>For a device in the field, <code>panic-reset</code> plus a watchdog is usually correct: a\nreboot recovers, a halt bricks. Log the panic reason to a retained RAM region so\nyou can read it after the restart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-testing-gap-and-how-to-close-it\">The testing gap, and how to close it</h2>\n<p>Firmware is famously untestable. Three techniques that work:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Separate logic from hardware.</strong> Put the state machine in a <code>no_std</code> crate with\nno HAL dependency, test it on the host with <code>cargo test</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fake the HAL.</strong> Implement <code>embedded-hal</code> traits for a mock sensor that\nreturns scripted values, including errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Run the firmware in QEMU in CI.</strong> <code>qemu-system-arm -machine lm3s6965evb</code> boots\na Cortex-M binary; <code>defmt</code> output can be asserted on.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure data-rehype-pretty-code-figure=\"\"><pre tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"rust\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\"><code data-language=\"rust\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\" style=\"display: grid;\"><span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">#[cfg(test)]</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">mod</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#F69D50\"> tests</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\"> {</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">    use</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#6CB6FF\"> super</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">::*</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">;</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"> </span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">    struct</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#F69D50\"> FlakySensor</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\"> { calls</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">:</span><span style=\"--shiki-ligh